---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/post-purchase';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/post-purchase');
const desc = 'Plan post-purchase SMS around payment, delivery, recipient permission, support exits, and incremental contribution.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/use-cases" class="text-sm font-bold text-orange-700 hover:text-slate-950">← All use cases</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Post-purchase education</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Post-purchase SMS starts with the order, not a timer</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">Help a customer use what arrived, resolve what did not, and choose whether another message is useful. A purchase is the beginning of several possible journeys, not permission to start every promotional flow.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Compare app options</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">Coordinate email and SMS</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Keep five kinds of truth separate</h2>
      <p>Use a store-scoped order ID, line-item IDs, fulfillment or shipment IDs, payment transaction IDs, and return/refund IDs. A customer profile joins these records; it does not replace them. Record source, event ID, effective time, received time, and version where available. Assign an owner for conflicting records before enabling sends.</p>
      <p>Shopify documents separate order, payment, fulfillment, and return statuses. Paid can include an order manually marked as paid; fulfillment is not evidence that a parcel reached the recipient. Use the <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/fulfillment/managing-orders/order-status">official order-status definitions</a> when mapping your store's events.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Authority to assign</th><th>Message boundary</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Order and cancellation</td><td>Commerce order record and approved edits</td><td>Created is not paid; archived is not proof of delivery. Canceling retires queued product promotions for canceled lines.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Payment</td><td>Payment ledger reconciled with the order</td><td>Authorization, capture, failure, and refund are separate. Never infer a successful charge from an order event.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Fulfillment</td><td>Warehouse or fulfillment record mapped to line quantities</td><td>A label or partial shipment cannot unlock whole-order arrival copy.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Delivery</td><td>Shipment tracking plus customer/support corrections</td><td>A carrier delivery scan is evidence, not certainty that the intended person has the goods. A nonreceipt report blocks celebratory follow-ups.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Return and refund</td><td>Return workflow and payment refund ledger respectively</td><td>A return request is not a completed refund. Communicate the actual stage without promising a settlement date.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Choose the recipient before the channel</h2>
      <p>The payer, account holder, shipping recipient, and SMS subscriber may be different people. Match the intended job to a verified contact and its permission record. Do not send a gift recipient the payer's receipt, price, or account link, or treat a shipping phone number as a marketing subscription. Keep shared numbers and profile merges in an exception queue when identity is uncertain.</p>
      <p>Classify each template and its actual content. An order update should explain that order; a coupon, cross-sell, review solicitation, or loyalty invitation needs its own permission and policy assessment. Adding an offer to a service template changes the decision. Neither a purchase nor a transactional label establishes authorization. Apply destination-specific requirements and sender rules with qualified review; this playbook is not a legal assurance.</p>
      <p>Retain the disclosure version, scope, source, timestamp, channel, destination, and subsequent withdrawals. Recheck permission and applicable sending restrictions at dispatch. Route opt-outs and help replies to working handlers; avoid routing around an opt-out through another sender. See <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent controls</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet-hour planning</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Build line-level education, not a universal schedule</h2>
      <p>Send setup instructions when the relevant item can reasonably be used and the recipient is eligible. For installation-dependent goods, an installation confirmation may be a better signal than delivery. For digital goods, access activation may matter more than shipping. Keep detailed instructions in an accessible email or account page; use SMS for a useful brief prompt only when its role and permission are clear.</p>
      <p>For a split order, let each delivered line unlock only its own education. Keep the backordered remainder on its service path. A replacement shipment should link to the original issue and avoid restarting the entire welcome sequence. A refund on one line should suppress related offers without erasing necessary updates for the retained items.</p>
      <p>For gifts, invite the purchaser to share a nonpersonal care guide instead of enrolling the recipient. For subscriptions, join the subscription contract and renewal cycle to the actual order. A renewal notice is not a new-customer onboarding trigger. Paused, canceled, skipped, payment-failed, and prepaid future shipments need distinct handling; do not suggest a refill while an existing renewal is unresolved.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Make support and cancellation real exits</h2>
      <p>Before each optional send, check open support cases, nonreceipt reports, return requests, cancellations, refunds, opt-outs, and relevant new purchases. Give a support problem precedence over a review request or cross-sell. Keep necessary factual service updates available through an appropriate authorized route; a marketing holdout must not withhold an order receipt or a required issue update.</p>
      <p>A support resolution does not automatically restart old jobs. Reconcile the current order and item state, confirm permission, and decide whether education still helps. Expose a usable support route and the store's actual return or cancellation instructions in the destination page. Do not promise a cancellation after dispatch or a refund before approval. Retain a reason code for every suppressed or retired job so an operator can explain the result.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Retire stale jobs before recovering the queue</h2>
      <p>Use an idempotency key built from store, order, line or shipment, recipient, message purpose, and journey version. Deduplicate provider retries as well as repeated source events. A timestamp alone is not a safe ordering rule: delayed delivery events can arrive after a return request. Re-read authoritative current state at dispatch instead of trusting the payload that originally scheduled the job.</p>
      <p>Set a purpose-specific expiry and a maximum data-age policy. If identity, permission, or shipment state cannot be verified, park the job with an owner and no automatic promotional fallback. After an outage, reconcile affected orders, remove canceled and superseded jobs, apply expiry, and restore only still-useful messages at a controlled rate. Do not replay an accumulated sequence as a burst.</p>
      <p>Record a send ledger and reconcile ambiguous provider responses before retrying; an API timeout does not prove the message failed. Maintain a kill switch per journey and sender. Retire the old queue when changing providers or templates, carry suppression and send history forward, and allow one owner to release recovered work. Compare intended sends with accepted and delivered statuses without treating any status as proof that a customer read the message.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Ask for a review only after a fair opportunity to use the item</h2>
      <p>A delivery estimate cannot start a review timer reliably. Require the relevant arrival or activation evidence, a product-appropriate opportunity for use, no unresolved issue, and the required permission. Invite honest feedback without filtering requests to predicted happy customers. If a return opens while the request waits, retire it and route the customer to support.</p>
      <p>Cross-sells need a separate decision: compatible retained item, available stock, no pending exchange that changes compatibility, and an audience whose permission covers the offer. Suppress products already bought or supplied by an active subscription. A subsequent purchase exits that offer queue. Do not attach a discount by default; compare the incremental contribution with a no-offer alternative. See <a class="link" href="/use-cases/reviews-and-ugc">review planning</a> and <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">replenishment</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Hypothetical proposed acceptance fixtures</h2>
      <p>These scenarios describe proposed operating checks, not merchant case studies or observed outcomes. Agree on the expected behavior with fulfillment, support, and channel owners before turning on a journey.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Pending payment:</strong> an order-created event arrives with an uncaptured authorization. Expect no “payment received” text and no paid-customer promotion until the relevant current state qualifies.</li>
        <li><strong>Two parcels:</strong> one of two line groups arrives. Expect education only for those delivered items; no whole-order review request while the second parcel is unresolved.</li>
        <li><strong>Gift:</strong> payer opts into marketing but the recipient supplies only a delivery number. Expect no marketing enrollment or price disclosure to that recipient.</li>
        <li><strong>Return overtakes delivery:</strong> a delayed delivery event arrives after a return request. Expect the current return state to block the waiting review and related cross-sell.</li>
        <li><strong>Subscription renewal:</strong> a prepaid future shipment is scheduled while another renewal payment fails. Expect cycle-specific service handling, no duplicate first-order onboarding, and no generic refill offer.</li>
        <li><strong>Duplicate callback:</strong> the same shipment event arrives twice and a provider request times out. Expect one logical job and reconciliation of send status before any retry.</li>
        <li><strong>Partial refund:</strong> a refunded accessory shares an order with a retained device. Expect accessory offers retired while relevant device education remains independently eligible.</li>
        <li><strong>Outage recovery:</strong> a canceled order, an expired setup tip, and a still-useful care guide wait in a restored queue. Expect the first two retired and the guide re-evaluated against current recipient, support, and permission state.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Choose a stack by the state it can prove</h2>
      <p>A unified email/SMS workflow can fit when the same team can demonstrate shared identities, exits, permission, and send history. Separate senders can fit when an orchestration owner can propagate those decisions and reconcile failures across both. An account or email-led program can fit detailed care instructions when SMS adds little utility. These are conditional designs, not supplier rankings.</p>
      <p>Use the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> and <a class="link" href="/compare">comparisons</a> to form a shortlist, then ask each candidate to demonstrate these scenarios with your actual integration and plan. Verify line-level payloads, late-event handling, support-state availability, permission exports, cancellation latency, reply routing, send-log access, and billing for retries or message segments. A feature name does not establish that your connector supplies the needed evidence.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Measure contribution and service effects separately</h2>
      <p>Randomize eligible customers into stable optional-message treatment and holdout groups; keep required service communications the same. Assign at customer level to reduce spillover across orders. Define eligibility, observation window, refund maturity, costs, and stop rules before launch. Report delivery problems, support contacts, opt-outs, and returns alongside commercial results. A reduction in replies alone could mean customers could not reach support.</p>
      <p>Hypothetical worksheet: 1,000 eligible customers in each group produce $2,000 treatment contribution and $1,700 holdout contribution after product cost, discounts, fulfillment, and matured returns. The difference is $0.30 per eligible customer, or $300 for the treatment group. Subtract $120 of incremental messaging and platform cost plus $50 of incremental support cost: $130 remains. Count each cost once; these inputs are illustrative, not a forecast.</p>
      <p>Include uncertainty and the cost of operating the workflow. Do not call platform-attributed repeat revenue incremental lift. If the sample or return window is insufficient, report the result as inconclusive. Keep service outcomes separate from optional selling experiments and revisit the <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">cost worksheet</a> when volume or integration work changes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
